<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Ocean Life and Conservation</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Plants That Live in the Ocean</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Participate in conflict resolution strategies with peers. Concept: Simplify complex concepts for group comprehension.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can help resolve disagreements on marine conservation ideas by encouraging active listening and respectful turns (e.g., suggests that each student listens fully before responding.)
Can simplify ocean ecosystem concepts, using visuals and gestures (e.g., uses hand motions to show how coral reefs provide homes for fish.)
Can clarify marine terms in both English and a familiar language for better understanding (e.g., explains "ecosystem" in simple words and both languages if needed.)</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different ocean plants (e.g., various types of seaweed, kelp forests, mangroves). Students work in pairs to quickly list adjectives to describe each plant and explain its general purpose in the ocean ecosystem. (e.g., "Mangroves are crucial habitats," "Seaweed is food for many species.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher presents a complex sentence: "The intricate interconnectedness of marine biodiversity ensures the ecological resilience of coastal areas." The teacher then models how to simplify it: "Many different kinds of life in the ocean help coastal areas stay strong and healthy." The teacher also models how to clarify a term: "Biodiversity means having many different types of living things. For example, many different fish, plants, and animals in one place." Teacher models phrases for conflict resolution: "Let's listen to both suggestions," or "How can we make this idea simpler for everyone?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'simplify,' do I mean make it more complicated or easier to understand?" (Expected answer: "Easier to understand.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'mediate' a disagreement? Is it taking one side or helping everyone agree?" (Expected answer: "Helping everyone agree.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students watch a short (2-3 minute) Public Service Announcement (PSA) video about a proposed marine protected zone to safeguard endangered mangrove forests and coral reefs in a coastal area. The video may present different perspectives or explain the scientific benefits in a slightly complex way. (Genre: Educational video; Register: Informative; Format: Short video clip).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Provide suggestions during discussions on marine protection and participate in debates on environmental conservation strategies (e.g., "We should establish more marine protected areas to help preserve ocean biodiversity.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify the main problem presented in the PSA (e.g., "Pollution is harming the coral reefs and mangroves.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify key terms mentioned (e.g., "biodiversity," "ecosystem," "protected zones").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In groups, they discuss one aspect of the PSA that might be difficult for someone to understand and brainstorm how to simplify it.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice simplifying complex concepts related to marine conservation, using present simple/continuous, and modals for suggestions/obligations. They will use vocabulary such as <em>ecosystem, biodiversity, protected zones, mangroves, conservation, preserve, sustain</em>. Pronunciation focus on stress in words like "ecosystem" and "biodiversity." Pragmatic functions of proposing conservation actions and describing ecological importance, and sociolinguistic skill of providing suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Concept Simplification Cloze:</b> The teacher provides a handout with 3-4 complex sentences about ocean plants or conservation challenges (e.g., "Photosynthesis in marine plants contributes significantly to global oxygen production."). Students work individually to complete cloze blanks in a simplified version of each sentence, using words from a word bank (e.g., "Ocean plants ____ a lot of oxygen for the world."). This focuses on simplifying specific terms.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Explain it Simply" Chain Drill:</b> In small groups, the teacher gives one student a complex statement about ocean conservation (e.g., "The overfishing of apex predators disrupts the trophic cascade within marine ecosystems."). That student explains it simply to the next student. The next student rephrases it, and the third student suggests a conservation action using a modal (e.g., "We should enforce stricter fishing rules."). They rotate roles and statements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mini-Disagreement Mediation:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides small index cards with two slightly conflicting opinions on a marine conservation idea (e.g., "Opinion A: We must ban all coastal development to protect marine life. Opinion B: We need some development for jobs, but it must be sustainable."). Students role-play trying to mediate the disagreement, using phrases like "Perhaps we can consider both sides," "Could you explain what you mean by 'sustainable'?", "We should think about solutions that protect and provide for people."</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Ocean Conservation Campaign Presentation & Mediation Challenge</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in their pre-assigned groups (from the 21st Century Skills Project: Ocean Conservation Campaign) to present their campaign ideas. Each group's presentation must include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A clear explanation of their proposed conservation action, using modals (e.g., "We have to protect our oceans by reducing plastic waste.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One member must simplify a complex marine ecosystem concept (e.g., the role of *plankton* or *seaweed* in the food chain, or the impact of *currents* on *biodiversity*) for the audience, using visuals or gestures where appropriate.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">During a Q&A session, the teacher or another group will introduce a minor "disagreement" or a need for clarification related to their campaign. The group members must work together to mediate the disagreement (e.g., two students from the same group might have slightly different ideas on how to implement a solution, or an audience member might not understand a term like "fragile habitat"). They must encourage active listening and respectful turns, and clarify terms using simple language or a familiar language if necessary (e.g., explaining "ecosystem" in simple English and potentially Spanish).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in presenting campaign ideas, collaborative interaction in simplifying concepts and resolving minor disagreements, and effective communicative task completion (campaign explanation and mediation).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' performance during their Ocean Conservation Campaign presentation, specifically focusing on their ability to simplify concepts and mediate minor disagreements or clarifications during the Q&A.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist/Rubric):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Simplification:</b> Explains complex concepts clearly and simply, using A2.4 vocabulary (e.g., "fragile," "endangered," "sustainable"). Uses visuals/gestures effectively.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation Strategies:</b> Encourages active listening, suggests respectful turns, and helps resolve disagreements politely.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Language Use:</b> Uses present simple/continuous for descriptions and modals for suggestions/obligations correctly. Incorporates target vocabulary (e.g., *ecosystem, biodiversity, conservation, protect*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Generally intelligible, with attention to stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "ecosystem," "biodiversity").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Works effectively with group members to present and mediate.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief reflection form:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one complex concept you tried to simplify during our campaign presentation today? How well do you think you explained it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Describe a time you helped resolve a small disagreement or clarified something for a peer today. What language did you use?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did practicing mediation skills help our group work together more effectively on the Ocean Conservation Campaign?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In what real-life situations outside of school could you use the mediation skills (simplifying ideas, resolving disagreements) we practiced today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the mediation tasks (simplifying concepts, resolving disagreements) worked particularly well? Did students effectively use the target grammar and vocabulary for mediation?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation lessons or projects? (e.g., more practice with specific phrases for polite interruption or clarification, more explicit modeling of gestures for simplification).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Plants That Live in the Ocean" theme and the Ocean Conservation Campaign project. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, applying the collaborative and communication skills learned to new content. The mediation skills practiced will be valuable for any future group projects or discussions, encouraging students to be clear, concise, and empathetic communicators in English.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>